@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

#
# prima-materia — generalization: heat from breath and directed attention
#

pmc:InnerHeatFromBreathAndDirectedAttention a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Heat from a held breath and attention directed into the trunk"@en, "Wärme aus gehaltenem Atem und gerichteter Aufmerksamkeit"@de ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Practices that combine a retained or forcefully driven breath with attention directed to a named region of the trunk raise the practitioner's measured peripheral temperature above what the same person shows at rest under the same ambient conditions, and by more than the breathing alone produces in a person not trained in the practice."@en ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:TummoYieldsInnerHeat ,
                       pmc:MulabandhaYieldsInnerFire ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    skos:note "Two attested claims: the Tibetan tummo instruction and the root seal of the Haṭhayogapradīpikā. Two is the floor the class sets and not a recommendation, and this node stands on the floor."@en ;
    skos:note "The weakness is a route and it is stated rather than smoothed over. The Tibetan material received the Indic; these are not two independent witnesses but one transmission at two stations, and the count here is therefore one and not two. The class does not require independence, but a reader ordering the queue by the spread of the traditions under a generalization must see that this one has none."@en ;
    skos:note "It is nonetheless the most examinable generalization the holding can currently form, and for a reason that has nothing to do with the number of witnesses: it names a quantity an instrument reads. The two clauses of the statement are separately refutable — a rise that a matched untrained person shows from the breathing alone refutes the second without touching the first."@en ;
    skos:note "Two protocols in examinations/tummo-heat.ttl already address the tradition-level claim and the question whether the gentler form works without the forceful breath. Neither points here, and neither is extended by this node: a protocol on a generalization has to be devised against this wording, and the difference between the two levels is exactly what the wording adds."@en .
